the list of people who believe that  Donald Trump is not going to relinquish  control of the White House peacefully if  he loses the 2020 election is growing  and it includes at least one Republican  candidate now who happens to be running  against Donald Trump his name's William  weld he is a former governor here in  Massachusetts he previously was running  as a libertarian he now is a Republican  and he says that Donald Trump will not  leave voluntarily if he loses the 2020  election from Real Time with Bill Maher  Bill Maher asked if Trump loses do you  think he'll leave and William weld said  not voluntarily he'll have a run at  saying it was a rigged game so I'm not  leaving I don't think the military and  indeed even the Justice Department the  rank-and-file the investigative agencies  would stand for that in this country  I like that William weld added that last  piece because it's important for us on  the left and not that William weld is on  the left but for anyone on the left who  is even entertaining this idea of what  would Trump do would he try to hold on  to control if he lost the 2020 election  we are not like the crazy right  with the birther stuff and the Obama's  going to cancel the 2016 election stuff  which is why I think it's important to  point out that William weld is not  saying Trump would succeed at staying in  the White House even if he lost the 2020  election he might try to pull it or try  to delay it but ultimately the centers  of power in the country wouldn't allow  it and I also think that that's the most  likely scenario and every single time  that this comes up I go out of my way  not to be hyperbolic because with Obama  there was so much nonsense about Obama  won't leave Obama will cancel the 2016  election and declare himself indefinite  president or Obama will declare martial  law as a justification for remaining  president beyond his eight years we want  to be a sober and realistic as possible  about this with Barack Obama  there was no indication whatsoever that  Barack Obama had those sort of  anti-democratic authoritarian tendencies  with regard to the presidency in trying  to not relinquish power  there was nothing Obama said which  suggested that he might out of his own  concoction not accept the raziel  of an election so when the right said  Obama won't leave it was 110 percent BS  with Trump it's not a hundred and ten  percent BS it's still not likely that it  would happen though right Trump has all  the way back to 2016 in the debates said  maybe he would accept the election  results maybe he wouldn't if they showed  that he lost depending on what he thinks  about the election results Trump has  said president for life  like what China made possible recently  is interesting and maybe we would try it  sometime  Trump has said years ahead of the 2020  election two years ahead that the only  way Democrats can defeat him in 2020 is  via fraud that is yet another one of  these sort of pretexts to the idea that  he might not accept the election results  Trump has lied about the validity of  past elections remember he claimed three  to five million people voted illegally  in 2016 for Hillary Clinton and that in  reality if you account for those three  to five million alleged illegal votes he  won the popular vote as well as the  electoral vote Trump has made clear that  he has a degree of narcissism that might  make him unable emotionally to accept  defeat so when people talk about Trump  won't leave the White House peacefully  if he loses is it more likely that he  would try to retain power than would  someone like Barack Obama obviously the  answer is yes is it likely Trump would  succeed if he tried at not relinquishing  the White House I don't think it's very  likely so what we need to do is let's  just work on voting him out rather than  focusing on whether or not he would  leave let's work on preventing voter  suppression and manipulation tactics to  actually hand Donald Trump the victory  because much more likely than Trump  loses and somehow stays in the White  House is that the combination of voter  ID laws and election machine tampering  and foreign influence and reductions in  voting hours and closures of polling  places all together push Donald Trump  into victory that's what we need to most  proactively be working to prevent I  agree with William weld that if Trump  tries not to give up  roll of the white house that the forces  of power I know that the Justice  Department's in the tank forum I know  all of that stuff but I think that that  would even be too far I think that he  would ultimately be removed and there's  a really big problem in American culture  today American political culture today  that this relates to expectations  expectations aren't being managed  properly so take impeachment for example  which will talk about the expectation  shouldn't be can we remove Trump via  impeachment by the 2020 election because  that's not happening the expectation  should be can impeachment proceedings  expose the content of the Moller report  enough to damage Trump to make sure that  he loses the 2020 election we have to  manage our expectations and similarly if  Trump loses the 2020 election and tries  to stay in power could he actually do it  I believe William weld is right and the  answer is no that he would be removed  but he might try to argue that the  election was bogus that it was rigged  that it was fraudulent whatever because  he's done it before in an election he  won but who knows what he would do in an  election that he loses last thing on  this the idea that the reason Trump  would be so adamant about not losing has  been floated as maybe being the statute  of limitations on obstruction there's  this idea that Trump it's not so much  that he wants to be president for  another four years although his ego  would take a hit if he were to lose but  it's the idea that if he is not  president for another four years he  could end up indicted for obstruction  because the statute of limitations would  not have expired there is a way around  that which would be if Trump loses the  2020 election three days before the  inauguration of the next president Trump  resigns Mike Pence becomes president and  issues a pre-emptive pardon for Donald  Trump it might work it might not the  legal experts have sort of different  views on that but there is a path even  if Trump loses in 2020 to protecting  himself from indictment on obstruction  after he's gone  just something to keep in mind
 

